[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Data Types in C#"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"In C#, data types are used to specify the type of value that a variable can hold. C# is a strongly-typed language, which means that the type of a variable must be declared before it can be used, and the compiler enforces type safety. Data types can be broadly classified into two categories: Primitive (Value) Types and Reference Types."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Primitive (Value) Types"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Primitive types are predefined by the C# language and represent simple values. Variables of primitive types directly contain their data. When you assign a primitive type variable to another, a copy of the value is created. These types are stored on the stack."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Integral Types: Represent whole numbers (without fractional parts)."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Floating-Point Types: Represent numbers with fractional parts."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Boolean Type: Represents a true or false value."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Character Type: Represents a single Unicode character."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Common Primitive Types"},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"int: Represents a 32-bit signed integer (e.g., -2147483648 to 2147483647)."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"float: Represents a 32-bit single-precision floating-point number."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"double: Represents a 64-bit double-precision floating-point number."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"bool: Represents a boolean value (true or false)."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"char: Represents a single Unicode character."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"decimal: Represents a 128-bit data type suitable for financial and monetary calculations."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"int age = 30; 
float price = 19.99f; 
bool isEmployed = true; 
char grade = 'A'; 
decimal salary = 50000.00m;"},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Reference Types"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"Reference types do not store the actual data directly. Instead, they store a reference (a pointer or memory address) to the location where the data is stored. When you assign a reference type variable to another, you are copying the reference, not the actual data. Both variables will then point to the same memory location. These types are stored on the heap."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Classes: User-defined types that can contain data (fields) and behavior (methods)."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Interfaces: Define a contract that classes can implement."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Arrays: Collections of elements of the same type."},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Delegates: Type-safe function pointers."},{"order":23,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Strings: Represents a sequence of characters."},{"order":24,"type":"subheading","text":"Common Reference Types"},{"order":25,"type":"bullet_point","text":"string: Represents a sequence of characters (e.g., "Hello, World!")."},{"order":26,"type":"bullet_point","text":"object: The base class for all other types in C#."},{"order":27,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Arrays (e.g., int[], string[])."},{"order":28,"type":"code","text":"string message = "Hello, C#!";
object obj = new object();
int[] numbers = { 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 };"},{"order":29,"type":"subheading","text":"Value vs. Reference Type Assignment"},{"order":30,"type":"paragraph","text":"Understanding the difference between value and reference type assignment is crucial for avoiding unexpected behavior in your code."},{"order":31,"type":"code","text":"// Value Type Assignment
int x = 10;
int y = x; // y gets a copy of x's value (10)
x = 20; // Changing x does not affect y
Console.WriteLine("x: " + x); // Output: x: 20
Console.WriteLine("y: " + y); // Output: y: 10

// Reference Type Assignment
int[] arr1 = { 1, 2, 3 };
int[] arr2 = arr1; // arr2 gets a reference to the same array as arr1
arr1[0] = 100; // Changing arr1 also affects arr2
Console.WriteLine("arr1[0]: " + arr1[0]); // Output: arr1[0]: 100
Console.WriteLine("arr2[0]: " + arr2[0]); // Output: arr2[0]: 100"},{"order":32,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":33,"type":"paragraph","text":"C# provides a rich set of data types that allow you to represent different kinds of data in your programs. Understanding the distinction between primitive (value) types and reference types is essential for writing correct and efficient C# code. Remember that value types store data directly, while reference types store a reference to the data."}]
